# Smart Fella or Fart Smella — website environment variables
#
# The marketing + quiz site itself requires NO environment variables to build,
# run, or deploy. The variables below are ONLY for the SFFS Creator Studio
# TikTok integration (app/tiktok + app/api/tiktok/*).
#
# Put REAL values in .env.local (gitignored) for local dev, and in the Vercel
# project settings for production. NEVER commit real secrets here.
# Prefix any browser-exposed variable with NEXT_PUBLIC_ (none are needed here).

# --- TikTok Content Posting API (required for /tiktok) ---
# From the TikTok for Developers portal → your app → Credentials.
TIKTOK_CLIENT_KEY=
TIKTOK_CLIENT_SECRET=

# --- Optional ---
# Override the OAuth redirect URI. Defaults to the production callback:
#   https://www.smartfellaorfartsmella.com/api/tiktok/callback
# Set this for local dev (e.g. http://localhost:3000/api/tiktok/callback) and
# register the SAME value in the TikTok portal.
# TIKTOK_REDIRECT_URI=

# Optional dedicated secret used to encrypt the stored TikTok session cookie.
# If unset, the encryption key is derived from TIKTOK_CLIENT_SECRET. Set any
# long random string (e.g. `openssl rand -hex 32`) to decouple token encryption
# from the client secret.
# TIKTOK_TOKEN_SECRET=

# --- Email lead capture ("get access" form) — AWS Lambda -> Aurora proxy ---
# Required for the /api/access-signup route. SERVER-SIDE ONLY — never prefix with
# NEXT_PUBLIC. The route POSTs validated leads to a small AWS Lambda (fronted by
# API Gateway) that inserts into Aurora (sffs.email_signups) via the RDS Data API.
# The ONLY secret this app holds is EMAIL_PROXY_SECRET (a random shared secret);
# NO AWS keys are used. Inserts happen in app/api/access-signup via
# lib/email-store.ts.
EMAIL_PROXY_URL=
EMAIL_PROXY_SECRET=

# --- PostHog product analytics (client + server capture) ---
# The PROJECT (public) key — safe to expose in the browser bundle. Used by
# posthog-js (client) AND posthog-node (server-side email_captured). Project 524578,
# US cloud. The client talks to the /ingest reverse proxy (next.config.ts rewrites)
# to dodge ad-blockers; NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST is the PostHog APP host (ui_host)
# for "view in PostHog" links only.
#
# ⚠️ The phx_ PERSONAL API key (PostHog management / MCP) is SERVER-MANAGEMENT ONLY.
# It must NEVER live in this repo or any app env — not even non-public. It stays in
# the MCP client config. Do not add a POSTHOG_PERSONAL_API_KEY here.
NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY=
NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST=https://us.posthog.com

# --- Resend (transactional email) ---
# Sends the "view your results" link after someone finishes the test.
# SERVER-ONLY. Deliberately NOT NEXT_PUBLIC_: it must never reach the browser
# bundle. lib/email/resend.ts is the only module that reads it, and it is
# imported with `server-only` so a client import is a build error.
RESEND_API_KEY=

# The From header. Until the sending domain is DNS-verified in Resend, this has
# to stay the shared sandbox sender, which can ONLY deliver to the Resend
# account owner's own address:
#   RESEND_FROM="Smart Fella <onboarding@resend.dev>"
# Once smartfellaorfartsmella.com is verified, switching to a real sender is a
# change to THIS VALUE ONLY, no code change:
#   RESEND_FROM="Smart Fella <results@smartfellaorfartsmella.com>"
# See docs/test-content/resend-domain-setup.md for the DNS records needed.
RESEND_FROM="Smart Fella <onboarding@resend.dev>"

# Absolute origin used to build the results link inside the email. Must be a
# real, reachable origin: a relative path is not clickable in an inbox.
RESULTS_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# --- Results link signing ---
# HMAC key for the self-contained results token. The finished test is encoded
# INTO the link and signed with this, so any instance can verify any link with
# no shared state; see lib/test/result-token.ts.
#
# REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION. A production deployment throws rather than falling
# back, because a signing key that quietly degrades to a public default is worse
# than one that is missing. Locally, leaving it blank uses a public development
# key and warns.
#
# ROTATING IT INVALIDATES EVERY LINK ALREADY SITTING IN AN INBOX, for up to the
# twelve months a result is valid. Treat it as permanent.
#   openssl rand -base64 32
RESULTS_TOKEN_SECRET=

# --- Durable result rows (aggregate only, no email address) ---
# Endpoint for sffs.test_results, the rows a per-grade-band percentile would be
# built from. Uses the same x-shared-secret header as the signup proxy.
#
# NOT YET BUILT. The signup Lambda validates an email before anything else, so
# it cannot carry these rows, and giving it them would attach an address to a
# result — the one thing the privacy page promises does not happen. Until this
# exists, a production deployment logs an error on the first finished test
# rather than dropping the rows silently. See lib/test/result-stats.ts.
RESULTS_STATS_URL=

# --- Draining results emails an outage swallowed ---
# The shared secret for POST /api/test-results/drain, which works through the
# `pending` rows the send route writes before it calls Resend and mails the
# people an outage lost. See app/api/test-results/drain/route.ts.
#
# UNSET MEANS THE ROUTE IS OFF. It answers 401 to everything, which is the right
# default for a public endpoint whose job is sending real mail to real people.
# Deliberately NOT the same value as EMAIL_PROXY_SECRET: this one gets typed
# into a terminal by a human during an incident and ends up in shell history,
# and it must not also be the credential that writes to Aurora.
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#
# Two things have to be true before a drain can do anything: this, and the
# `pending_sends` branch of the email proxy Lambda actually being deployed (see
# infra/lambda/README.md). Without the second, the route reports itself
# unavailable rather than reporting an empty queue.
RESULTS_DRAIN_SECRET=

# --- The scheduled drain (Vercel Cron) ---
# The drain runs itself every five minutes now; see the schedule in vercel.json
# and the reasoning in app/api/test-results/drain/route.ts. Cron cannot send a
# custom header, so it authenticates with this instead: when CRON_SECRET is set,
# Vercel attaches `Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET` to every cron invocation,
# and the route's GET handler accepts nothing else.
#
# UNSET MEANS THE SCHEDULE IS INERT. The cron still fires and the route still
# answers 401, which fails closed but fails SILENTLY — the backlog simply never
# drains and nothing says so. If you are wondering why a backlog is sitting
# still, check this before anything else.
#
# Separate from RESULTS_DRAIN_SECRET on purpose: one is held by a person, one by
# the platform, and leaking either should not imply the other.
#   openssl rand -base64 32
CRON_SECRET=

# --- Which version of the site is live at / ---
# Three homepages live in the tree at once and this picks between them. Only
# `/` changes: /privacy, /terms, /support, /about, /app-store-copy, /internal
# and every redirect are live in all three.
#
#   v1  the original multi-section marketing site
#   v2  the single-screen waitlist page (what is on production today)
#   v3  the Official Smart Fella Test flow  (default)
#
# This is a rollback lever, not just a convenience: if v3 converts worse than
# v2, putting v2 back is this one value and a redeploy, rather than reverting a
# large branch under pressure.
#
# An unrecognised value logs a warning and falls back to v3 rather than
# throwing — a typo showing the current site beats a typo showing no site.
#
# In DEVELOPMENT ONLY, `?v=1` / `?v=2` / `?v=3` on the URL overrides this
# without a server restart, which turns comparing the three into three tabs.
# The override is gated on NODE_ENV for the same reason the dev tools are.
SITE_VERSION=v3
